<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Shanghai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> was fought from August to November 1937 in and around Shanghai, the first major battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> committed many of his best German-trained divisions, which held out for three months at a cost of very heavy casualties. Japanese landings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> Bay forced a Chinese retreat that opened the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-shanghai.jpg" alt="Battle of Shanghai" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shanghai1937KMT_machine_gun_nest.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Armed conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had continued without a declaration of war since the Japanese invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931 and the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1932, and it escalated into a full-scale war after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo-bridge-incident">Marco Polo Bridge Incident</a> of July 1937. Shanghai was China's largest and most cosmopolitan city and its main commercial hub and port. By starting a major battle there, the Chinese leadership is believed to have aimed to divert Japan to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> delta, to gain time to move industry inland and to draw Western powers to its side, though there is no consensus on whether this succeeded. The last negotiations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> failed over Japan's demand for a Chinese withdrawal from the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The battle lasted from 13 August to 26 November 1937 and involved around one million troops. In the first stage, to 22 August, the Chinese army besieged the Japanese Naval Landing Force in street fighting. On 23 August the Japanese 3rd and 11th Divisions landed at Chuanshakou, Shizilin and Baoshan on the northeast coast, which stretched the Chinese front. The armies then fought house to house in the creek country north of the city, around Luodian and Dachang, while the fighting in the city became a stalemate.</p><p>After Dachang fell the Chinese began withdrawing from the city on the night of 26 October, leaving one battalion of the 88th Division to hold the Sihang Warehouse. Japanese landings at Jinshanwei on Hangzhou Bay, planned since 12 October, forced the Chinese to retire from the whole front, and a general retreat was ordered on 8 November. The Japanese army began its advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> on 11 November, covering nearly 400 kilometres in about a month.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Japanese took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> after over three months of fighting, a campaign they had expected to finish in days. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> lost a large part of its best troops, the elite divisions trained and equipped by the Germans. No foreign power intervened. A Chinese historian of the time called it the bloodiest battle since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, though estimates of casualties differ. According to Iwane Matsui's diary, all 500 Chinese prisoners taken near Wusong were shot; both sides accused each other of using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a>, and Japanese forces are confirmed to have used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a> at least thirteen times. Peter Harmsen argues that the battle presaged urban combat in the rest of the century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Shanghai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chinese armies lost the <b>Battle of Shanghai</b>, Nanjing fell in December 1937 amid the Nanjing Massacre, and the government withdrew to Chongqing, where it held out until 1945 at a cost of millions of military and civilian lives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Military</a></li></ul></section>
